{
  "dom_id": "step-q332914",
  "name": "Question 2",
  "problem": "Consider the following inhomogeneous system of linear differential equations:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]x(t)+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThe complementary solution to this system is given by\n\n$$\n{x}_{c}(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3t} \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ {e}^{5t}\\end{aligned}],\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThen, the general solution of the system is\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td rowspan=\"2\" height=\"40px\" align=\"center\">\n$x(t)={x}_{c}(t)+$\n{{field-1}}\n$\\cdot \\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td width=\"5px\" align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"5px\" height=\"30px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that the general solution of an inhomogeneous system can be written as \n\n$$\nx(t)={x}_{c}(t)+{x}_{p}(t)\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{c}(t)$ is the complementary solution (solution of the corresponding homogeneous system) and ${x}_{p}(t)$ is a particular solution.\n\nThe forcing term\n\n$$\n{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nof the inhomogeneous system of ODEs is exponential. Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also exponential, i.e.\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nwhere $a,b$  are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the derivative of ${x}_{p}$ with respect to $t$ gives\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}^{′}(t)=-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the unknown constants, we substitute ${x}_{p}$ and ${x}_{p}^{′}$ into our system:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+{e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ -4[\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}a \\\\ b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a \\\\ 5b\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}-14 \\\\ -27\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-4a \\\\ -4b\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}3a-14 \\\\ 5b-27\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the corresponding components, we get the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}3a-14=-4a \\\\ 5b-27=-4b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n3a-14=-4a\\,⇒\\,a=2.\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n5b-27=-4b\\,⇒\\,b=3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution is\n\n$$\n{x}_{p}(t)={e}^{-4t}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$",
